Notes on Recode File

1.   HV002 - In Cameroon households were identified by a three digit
     structure number and a two digit household number.  These variables are
     stored as country specific variables SHSTRUCT and SHMENAGE.

2.   HV109 - Cases with either 6 or 7 years of primary education were
     classified as completed primary.

3.   HV023 - The sample was designed with three domains: Yaound‚+Douala,
     other urban and rural.  Each domain has its own distinct weight,
     however, the weights for 18 clusters were adjusted because of under
     coverage.  As a result there are 21 different weights in the data.

4.   V002 - In Cameroon households were identified by a three digit structure
     number and a two digit household number.  These variables are stored as
     country specific variables SSTRUCT and SMENAGE.

5.   V023 - see HV023.

6.   V139 - De jure region of residence was not determined for visitors.

7.   V149 - Cases with either 6 or 7 years of primary education were
     classified as completed primary.

8.   To merge the woman's individual data with the men's (SNMR21.DAT), the
     user should use the first 12 characters of CASEID concatenated to
     SMLIGNE read as 3 characters with leading blanks.  The recode files
     produce only 977 weighted couples if the woman's weight (V005/1000000)
     is used.  The user should be aware that there are 20 couples for which
     there is an inconsistency between the wife's report of husband line
     number and the husband's report of wife's line number.  If the user
     wishes to exclude those couples, the user should require that one of the
     occurrences of the husband variable HV034 equals the woman's line number
     (V003).

9.   The user should be aware that the husband's data are available within
     the woman's individual recode file and it is not necessary to merge the
     two recode files.  See REC99 and REC9A for these data.

Inconsistencies in the Data File

1.   HV006, HV007, HV008 -  There are 121 households with no date of
     interview.  These correspond to the 121 households with result of
     interview "other".  HV006 and HV007 are not applicable and HV007 is 9999
     for these households.

2.   HV220 - There is one household with head age 6.  All other heads of
     household are 14 years old and above.

3.   B3 - date of birth of child.  Five cases of births with "impossible"
     intervals were corrected by the recode program using reasonable values
     for the date of birth.  Other variables depending on B3 are changed as
     a result.

4.   From CMIR21 to CMIR22, variable HV118 was corrected for 41 men in the
     household (RECH1).  Of these changes, 18 men were corrected from "1"
     (eligible) to "0", and 23 were corrected from "0" (not eligible) to "1".
     The household variable HV011 (number of eligible men) was changed if
     necessary.MALE RECODE

